 </nav>
 <div class="prayer-knowledge-summary">
  <p><strong>Prayer begins</strong> when its window opens, which is what the calculator estimates. <strong>Jama'ah / iqamah</strong> is the later congregation time set by each mosque. <strong>Sunrise</strong> marks the end of the Fajr window; it is not a sixth obligatory prayer.</p>
 </div>
 <section id="prayer-five-windows" class="prayer-knowledge-section" aria-labelledby="prayer-five-title">
  <h3 id="prayer-five-title">When does each prayer begin and end?</h3>
  <p>The five daily prayers have appointed periods (<a href="https://quran.com/4/103" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 4:103</a>). The table describes the major solar markers; there are additional differences of interpretation about some boundaries, especially Asr and Isha.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="prayer-knowledge-table">
   <thead><tr><th scope="col">Prayer</th><th scope="col">Begins</th><th scope="col">What it means</th><th scope="col">End / next boundary</th></tr></thead>
   <tbody>{prayers.map(p=><tr><th scope="row">{p.name}</th><td>{p.start}</td><td>{p.meaning}</td><td>{p.end}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <div class="prayer-knowledge-cards">
   {prayers.map(p=><article><h4>{p.name}</h4><p>{p.detail}</p></article>)}
  </div>
  <p class="prayer-knowledge-source">The limits of the prayers are discussed in <a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:612a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 612a</a>. The narration also underscores that not every prayer's preferred or permitted end can be reduced to a single clock figure.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="prayer-methods-explained" class="prayer-knowledge-section" aria-labelledby="prayer-methods-title">
  <h3 id="prayer-methods-title">Why Fajr and Isha differ between apps and mosques</h3>
  <p>At dawn and dusk the Sun is still below the horizon. Many prayer timetables estimate the start of Fajr or Isha from the <strong>Sun's angle below the horizon</strong>. A larger Fajr angle generally produces an earlier dawn estimate. Different Isha angles and fixed-interval methods can produce noticeably different evening times.</p>
  <p>The table shows examples of well-known software presets. The figures are <em>method parameters</em>, not exact religious or observational guarantees for every region and date.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table class="prayer-knowledge-table">
   <thead><tr><th scope="col">Convention</th><th scope="col">Fajr</th><th scope="col">Isha</th><th scope="col">Context</th></tr></thead>
   <tbody>{conventions.map(m=><tr><th scope="row">{m.name}</th><td>{m.fajr}</td><td>{m.isha}</td><td>{m.note}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="prayer-knowledge-source">*The published Umm al-Qura convention includes a longer Isha interval during Ramadan. The underlying library may not automatically apply that seasonal adjustment; use an authoritative local timetable during Ramadan. See the <a href="https://github.com/batoulapps/adhan-js/blob/develop/METHODS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adhan calculation parameters</a>, <a href="https://fiqhcouncil.org/the-suggested-calculation-method-for-fajr-and-isha/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fiqh Council of North America</a> and <a href="https://praytimes.org/docs/methods" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PrayTimes method comparisons</a>.</p>
  <h4>Which method should I choose?</h4>
  <p>Start with the method published by the mosque or religious authority you follow. Check the selected city, date, time zone and Asr setting first. Then use <strong>Compare calculation methods</strong> above to see how another convention would affect Fajr and Isha on the same day. When a high-latitude rule limits twilight times, both methods may even return the same time despite having different angles. A displayed difference does not by itself prove that either authority is wrong.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="prayer-asr-differences" class="prayer-knowledge-section" aria-labelledby="prayer-asr-title">
  <h3 id="prayer-asr-title">Standard and Hanafi Asr — why are there two times?</h3>
  <div class="prayer-asr-grid">
   <div><h4>Standard Asr</h4><p>Uses a shadow length equivalent to the object's length <strong>in addition to</strong> its shadow at solar noon. This convention is associated with Shafi'i, Maliki and Hanbali calculations.</p></div>
   <div><h4>Hanafi Asr</h4><p>Uses a shadow length equivalent to <strong>twice</strong> the object's length in addition to its noon shadow. This gives a later calculated Asr start.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p>Choose the Asr school followed by your mosque. Changing this option does not require changing your Fajr/Isha twilight convention. Details of scholarly interpretation go beyond a numerical timetable; compare the <a href="https://praytimes.org/docs/manual" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PrayTimes Asr rules</a>.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="prayer-high-latitude" class="prayer-knowledge-section" aria-labelledby="prayer-lat-title">
  <h3 id="prayer-lat-title">UK summer, Scotland and very high latitudes</h3>
  <p>In northern Britain and other high-latitude regions, the Sun may not reach the usual twilight angle on some summer nights. A normal Fajr or Isha calculation can therefore become impractical or undefined. Apps use different approximation rules for these conditions.</p>
  <ul>
   <li><strong>Seventh of the night:</strong> Uses a seventh of the sunset-to-sunrise interval near dawn or dusk.</li>
   <li><strong>Middle of the night:</strong> Uses the midpoint of that overnight interval as an alternative limit.</li>
   <li><strong>Twilight-angle fraction:</strong> Uses the selected Fajr and Isha angles to form an appropriate portion of the night.</li>
   <li><strong>Polar regions:</strong> When sunrise or sunset itself is absent, another special fallback may be needed. The calculator leaves such results unavailable unless you explicitly choose a fallback.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Our calculator offers these settings under <strong>High-latitude and polar calculation settings</strong>. Its default uses the Adhan library's location-based recommended rule, but that is not a declaration that the method matches the religious authority in your area. In Newcastle, Glasgow, Edinburgh, Aberdeen and further north, check your local mosque's summer timetable before relying on a calculated Fajr or Isha estimate.</p>
  <p class="prayer-knowledge-source">Reference: <a href="https://praytimes.org/docs/calculation" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">PrayTimes higher-latitude explanations</a> and <a href="https://github.com/batoulapps/adhan-js/blob/develop/METHODS.md" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Adhan high-latitude options</a>.</p>
 </section>
 <section id="prayer-mosque-times" class="prayer-knowledge-section" aria-labelledby="prayer-mosque-title">
  <h3 id="prayer-mosque-title">How to compare your mosque's timetable</h3>
  <ol class="prayer-checklist">
   <li><strong>Match the location and date.</strong> Even nearby towns may have slightly different solar times, and daylight-saving changes affect the clock.</li>
   <li><strong>Look for the column heading.</strong> A mosque may publish separate times for the prayer <em>beginning</em>, the <em>adhan</em> and the <em>iqamah / jama'ah</em>. Compare like with like.</li>
   <li><strong>Check the calculation convention.</strong> Compare Fajr and Isha angles or fixed-interval settings with those displayed by the calculator.</li>
   <li><strong>Check the Asr school.</strong> A standard/Hanafi mismatch can explain a large part of the afternoon difference.</li>
   <li><strong>Check seasonal and safety adjustments.</strong> Local authorities may use special Ramadan, high-latitude or precautionary timetables rather than a generic app preset.</li>
   <li><strong>Use the mosque's announcement for congregational prayer.</strong> Our calculator does not obtain or validate live mosque iqamah times.</li>
  </ol>
  <h4>How do time zones and daylight saving affect the result?</h4>
  <p>A proper solar calculation returns an instant in time; the selected location's time zone determines its wall-clock display. For example, <code>Europe/London</code> changes between GMT and BST. Do not manually add an hour to an already correctly formatted result. Travellers should choose the location where they will actually pray.</p>
  <h4>Are sunrise and Jumu'ah included?</h4>
  <p>Sunrise is provided as the end marker for Fajr, not another one of the five obligatory prayers. Friday Jumu'ah is not a separate astronomical event: the congregation and khutbah time is set by your mosque, usually within the Dhuhr period, with further religious conditions.</p>
  <h4>Can I print or download prayer times?</h4>
  <p>Use the calculator's <strong>Next 7 days</strong> section for a dated table, and its <strong>Download CSV</strong> option to keep or print the estimated times. If you share a timetable with a group, include the city, method and date, and do not label the calculated entries as official mosque jama'ah times.</p>
 </section>
